    "Full API spec": "pom.xml: Maven descriptor sets Java source/target 11, encodings to UTF-8, adds junit-jupiter-api and junit-jupiter-engine test dependencies, and configures maven-surefire-plugin to run JUnit 5 tests. No external runtime dependencies.\n\nsrc/main/java/Rect.java: public final class Rect { public final double x; public final double y; public final double width; public final double height; public Rect(double x, double y, double width, double height); public double left(); public double top(); public double right(); public double bottom(); Semantics: immutable AABB defined by top-left x,y and size. left()=x, top()=y, right()=x+width, bottom()=y+height.\n\nsrc/main/java/Ball.java: public final class Ball { private double x,y,vx,vy,radius; public Ball(double x, double y, double vx, double vy, double radius); public double getX(); public double getY(); public double getVx(); public double getVy(); public double getRadius(); void setPosition(double x, double y); void setVelocity(double vx, double vy); Semantics: setPosition and setVelocity are package-private (no access modifier) for tests; getters return current state; radius>0 invariant is assumed. No internal clamping.\n\nsrc/main/java/Paddle.java: public final class Paddle { private double x,y,width,height,maxX; public Paddle(double x, double y, double width, double height, double maxX); public double getX(); public double getY(); public double getWidth(); public double getHeight(); public void setX(double x); public Rect asRect(); Semantics: paddle uses center semantics (x,y at center). setX clamps the center x to [width/2, maxX - width/2]. asRect() returns Rect with top-left at (x - width/2, y - height/2) and same width/height.\n\nsrc/main/java/Brick.java: public final class Brick { private final Rect rect; private int hitPoints; private final boolean breakable; public Brick(Rect rect, int hitPoints, boolean breakable); public Rect getRect(); public int getHitPoints(); public boolean isBreakable(); public void hit(); public boolean isDestroyed(); Semantics: hit() decrements hitPoints by 1 if hitPoints>0 and always leaves hitPoints >= 0. isDestroyed() returns hitPoints == 0. Non-breakable bricks can be destroyed logically (hitPoints can reach 0) but are not removed nor scored by Game.\n\nsrc/main/java/Collision.java: public final class Collision { public static boolean circleIntersectsRect(double cx, double cy, double r, Rect rect); public static double[] penetration(double cx, double cy, double r, Rect rect); Semantics: Inclusive intersection: returns true if the distance from (cx,cy) to the rect is <= r. Implementation uses nearest-point-on-rect clamping and squared distance check. penetration() requires that an intersection exists; it returns new double[]{penX, penY} where penX = max(0, r - abs(cx - clamp(cx, rect.left(), rect.right()))), penY = max(0, r - abs(cy - clamp(cy, rect.top(), rect.bottom()))). These are magnitudes only. Callers choose the axis with the smaller magnitude to reflect velocity. In exact ties, callers may prefer Y-axis reflection for determinism.\n\nsrc/main/java/Game.java: public final class Game { private final double width,height; private final Paddle paddle; private final Ball ball; private final java.util.List<Brick> bricks; private int lives; int score; boolean levelCompleted; boolean lifeLost; public Game(double width, double height, Paddle paddle, Ball ball, java.util.List<Brick> bricks, int lives); public int getLives(); public boolean isLifeLost(); public boolean isLevelCompleted(); public int getScore(); public java.util.List<Brick> getBricks(); public Paddle getPaddle(); public Ball getBall(); public void tick(double dt); Semantics and rules: - Constructor: stores width/height; keeps references to the given paddle and ball; makes a defensive copy of the provided bricks list while retaining object identities, in insertion order; lives>=0. getBricks() returns an unmodifiable view of the internal list. - tick(dt): Validates dt is finite and > 0; otherwise throws IllegalArgumentException. On each call, resets lifeLost=false. Integrates the ball exactly once: ball.x += ball.vx * dt; ball.y += ball.vy * dt. Collision processing order: (1) walls (left/right/top reflection, bottom-out life loss), (2) paddle, (3) bricks (first-hit only). Walls: inclusive checks against bounds [0,width]x[0,height]. Left/right: if ball.x - r <= 0 reflect vx = -vx and clamp ball.x = r; if ball.x + r >= width reflect vx and clamp ball.x = width - r. Top: if ball.y - r <= 0 reflect vy and clamp ball.y = r. Bottom-out: if ball.y - r >= height then a life is lost: decrement lives exactly by 1 (but not below 0), set lifeLost=true, and reset ball state as follows: position x := paddle.getX(), y := paddle.getY() - paddle.getHeight()/2 - ball.getRadius() (exactly tangent above the paddle); velocity vx := 0; vy := -Math.abs(previous vy). No further collisions are processed in this tick when bottom-out occurs. Paddle collision: compute paddleRect = paddle.asRect(); if Collision.circleIntersectsRect(ball.x,ball.y,r,paddleRect) reflect along min-penetration axis. Resolution: obtain {penX,penY} = Collision.penetration(...). If penY <= penX reflect vertical (vy = -vy) and translate ball out along Y by penY in the sign away from the paddle; else reflect horizontal (vx = -vx) and translate along X accordingly. Bricks collision: iterate over a snapshot copy of the bricks list and handle the first brick for which there is an intersection similarly: reflect along min-penetration axis, call brick.hit(), and if brick.isBreakable() and brick.isDestroyed() then remove the brick from the internal list and increase score by exactly 100. Stop after handling the first colliding brick this tick. Level completion: levelCompleted is true iff all remaining bricks in the list are either non-breakable or destroyed (i.e., there are no remaining breakable and non-destroyed bricks). Flags and getters: getBricks() returns an unmodifiable list; lifeLost reflects only the outcome of the most recent tick; score is cumulative; getLives() returns current lives.\n\nsrc/main/java/Main.java: public final class Main { public static void main(String[] args); Semantics: constructs a tiny deterministic demo (e.g., a small world with a paddle, a ball, and zero or one brick), runs a single Game.tick with a fixed dt, and prints a single status line summarizing lives and score. No input reading, no GUI, and deterministic output.\n\nsrc/test/java/MainTest.java: JUnit 5 tests only. Tests import classes from the default package (no package declarations). Must not rely on GUI or timing. Covers: (1) Main.main runs without throwing; (2) one-brick fixture leads to brick removal, +100 score, and levelCompleted true; (3) life-loss fixture triggers at bottom-out, decrements lives once, sets lifeLost true, centers and positions ball exactly above paddle, and sets vx=0 and vy negative absolute of prior vy. Tests may access package-private Ball.setPosition and Ball.setVelocity to set up fixtures.\n\nImport/include rules: tests reference only the declared classes; core modules must not depend on Main; Game may depend on Collision; entities may be used by Game; no other cross-module cycles.",
    "Shared Knowledge": "Build with Maven; runtime entry is src/main/java/Main.java. All domain classes reside in the default package under src/main/java with no package declarations, allowing tests in the default package to access package-private methods. Core logic lives in Game, Ball, Paddle, Brick, Rect, Collision. Game.tick(dt) performs deterministic single-step integration and collision handling (walls, then paddle, then bricks), and enforces exact bottom-out life-loss/reset rules. Collision stays pure and stateless. Tests are headless and deterministic, use only JUnit Jupiter, and interact with default-package classes directly. getBricks() returns an unmodifiable view; Game holds a defensive copy of the bricks list.",
    "Execution Protocol": "Build and run tests with: mvn -q test. There is exactly one protocol-level test file at src/test/java/MainTest.java. Tests are headless and non-interactive. Runtime entry is Main.main in src/main/java/Main.java; tests ensure it executes without error. No additional files alter protocol-prescribed files.",
    "Anything UNCLEAR": ""
}
